{"birds": [{"name": "House Finch", "sci": "Haemorhous mexicanus", "n": 115, "first": "8:36 AM", "last": "1:33 PM", "ts": 1793626575, "mins": [516, 517, 517, 518, 518, 518, 519, 520, 520, 521, 521, 522, 523, 524, 526, 526, 528, 528, 529, 529, 530, 530, 531, 531, 531, 532, 534, 534, 534, 535, 535, 536, 536, 537, 538, 539, 539, 540, 540, 540, 541, 542, 542, 543, 544, 545, 545, 548, 553, 554, 555, 564, 566, 567, 568, 572, 573, 574, 577, 579, 580, 580, 581, 582, 582, 583, 583, 584, 584, 584, 586, 587, 588, 588, 589, 589, 591, 592, 596, 597, 597, 598, 598, 598, 599, 611, 622, 622, 623, 623, 624, 624, 628, 639, 646, 646, 647, 647, 647, 648, 658, 674, 686, 686, 687, 688, 688, 689, 754, 774, 774, 785, 806, 813, 813], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Native to the West, house finches were released on Long Island in 1940 as pet-shop “Hollywood finches” and spread across the East. Redder males are favored by females.", "vox": "sounds/voice/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113005", "snd": "sounds/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113108", "sndby": "Ted Floyd, xeno-canto XC622745, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "housefinch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/housefinch.png", "w": 800, "h": 581, "len": 662.0, "beak": [0.12, 0.13, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "B+AAAAfwAQoP8AHOH/gB7wf8Af8D/gH/B/8A3wf/AP4H/4zgB//fwAP/zwAD//4AAf/+AAD//AAAf/gAAD/8AA/+/AAH4B4AB+APAB6AD4A4AAeA8AAAAMAAAAA="}}, {"name": "Eastern Bluebird", "sci": "Sialia sialis", "n": 43, "first": "8:42 AM", "last": "2:08 PM", "ts": 1793626956, "mins": [522, 522, 523, 523, 525, 535, 535, 536, 539, 540, 541, 544, 545, 546, 570, 584, 604, 604, 609, 650, 652, 653, 708, 709, 710, 713, 714, 715, 715, 716, 716, 717, 723, 730, 733, 733, 734, 744, 752, 753, 839, 847, 848], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Bluebirds nest in tree cavities, and their numbers crashed in the early 1900s when starlings and house sparrows took the holes. Volunteer nest-box trails helped them rebound.", "vox": "sounds/voice/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113046", "snd": "sounds/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113130", "sndby": "Robin Carter, xeno-canto XC1374, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "bluebird", "cm": 18.0, "img": "cutouts/bluebird.png", "w": 800, "h": 609, "len": 744.0, "beak": [0.69, 0.1, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 24, "b": "AAPwAAAH/AAAD/4AAA/4AAAf8AAAH/AAAD/wAAB/+AAAf/gDAP/wDgH/8BwD//B4A//h4Af/54AH/94AD/98AB//4AA/P4AAPH0AAHPhAABvgAAA3gAAAPgAAABwAAAA"}}, {"name": "Blue Jay", "sci": "Cyanocitta cristata", "n": 7, "first": "8:43 AM", "last": "9:30 AM", "ts": 1793627038, "mins": [523, 568, 568, 568, 569, 569, 570], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "That blue isn’t a pigment. Tiny structures in the feathers scatter light, which is why a crushed blue feather looks brown. Jays also bury acorns and help spread oak trees.", "vox": "sounds/voice/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793112993", "snd": "sounds/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793113328", "sndby": "Christopher McPherson, xeno-canto XC691163, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "bluejay", "cm": 28.0, "img": "cutouts/bluejay.png", "w": 385, "h": 369, "len": 474.0, "beak": [0.99, 0.17, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 31, "b": "AAAPgAAAD+AAAA/4AAAH+AAAD/8AAB//AAA//gAAf/AAAP/wAAH/8AAD//AAB//wAB//4AAf/+AAf//gAH//zAD//5wA//94Af+OcAP/8eAD9/fgA/l/gAPgHwAHgD4AHwA4AB8AeAA+AGAAfADgAHgAwADwAAAA4AAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Fish Crow", "sci": "Corvus ossifragus", "n": 13, "first": "8:44 AM", "last": "10:48 AM", "ts": 1793627079, "mins": [524, 543, 543, 544, 544, 547, 547, 547, 635, 635, 635, 636, 648], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "It looks just like an American Crow. The giveaway is the voice: a nasal, two-note “uh-uh” instead of a clear “caw.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793112991", "snd": "sounds/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793113105", "sndby": "Mike Nelson, xeno-canto XC130581, CC BY-NC-SA 3.0", "key": "fishcrow", "cm": 38.0, "img": "cutouts/fishcrow.png", "w": 800, "h": 474, "len": 557.0, "beak": [0.27, 0.08, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 19, "b": "AH4AAAD+AAAAPwAAAB+AAAAf4AAAH/AAAB/4AAAf/gAAD/4AAAf/AAAD/4AAAf/AAAD34P8Bs+D///Dg/////v////8AD///AAAP/w=="}}, {"name": "Rusty Blackbird", "sci": "Euphagus carolinus", "n": 7, "first": "8:45 AM", "last": "12:05 PM", "ts": 1793627139, "mins": [525, 528, 558, 560, 560, 579, 725], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Rusty Blackbirds have undergone one of the steepest declines of any North American landbird, and scientists are still working out why. They forage in wet woods, flipping leaves along streams.", "vox": "sounds/voice/euphagus-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113002", "snd": "sounds/euphagus-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113205", "sndby": "Todd Wilson, xeno-canto XC63365, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "rustyblackbird", "cm": 23.0, "img": "cutouts/rustyblackbird.png", "w": 800, "h": 602, "len": 706.0, "beak": [0.44, 0.1, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 24, "b": "A/AAAAf8AAAH/AAAB/AAAA/4AAAP/AADD/4ADg//ABwP/4B4B//A4Af/w8AH/+8AA//8AAH/8AAA//gAAH/8AAA+/AAA/j4AAfAfAAfgD8AewAfAOAADwPAAAADgAAAA"}}, {"name": "Red-shouldered Hawk", "sci": "Buteo lineatus", "n": 4, "first": "8:47 AM", "last": "11:05 AM", "ts": 1793627226, "mins": [527, 527, 571, 665], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its loud, repeated kee-ah call is a favorite for Blue Jays to copy, so a hawk you hear in the yard may really be a jay.", "vox": "sounds/voice/buteo-lineatus.mp3?v=1793193504", "snd": "sounds/buteo-lineatus.mp3?v=1793193566", "sndby": "Denis Provencher, xeno-canto XC808846, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "redshouldered", "cm": 43.0, "img": "cutouts/redshouldered.png", "w": 523, "h": 800, "len": 863.0, "beak": [0.987, 0.093, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 49, "b": "AAAH+AAAD/4AAA/+AAAf/wAAP/8AAD//AAA/+AAAf/AAAP/4AAH/+AAB//gAA//4AAf/+AAP//gAH//8AD///AB///wAf//4AP//+AD///gB///wAf//8AP//+AD///gA///wAf//8AH//+AD///AA///gAP//4AH//8AB///AAf//wAH//4AB//+AA///gAP//4AH//8AD/9/gA/+f4AP/AewD/g//gPwf/4D4H/4B+B3+AfgAcAHwADAD8AAAAeAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Dark-eyed Junco", "sci": "Junco hyemalis", "n": 26, "first": "8:50 AM", "last": "2:08 PM", "ts": 1793627445, "mins": [530, 533, 534, 534, 534, 536, 537, 537, 546, 546, 574, 721, 731, 731, 794, 798, 811, 815, 818, 819, 819, 820, 846, 848, 848, 848], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Often called “snowbirds” because they show up as winter arrives. The white outer tail feathers flash as they flit away.", "vox": "sounds/voice/junco-hyemalis.mp3?v=1793113009", "snd": "sounds/junco-hyemalis.mp3?v=1793113154", "sndby": "Phoenix Birder, xeno-canto XC563632, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "junco", "cm": 15.5, "img": "cutouts/junco.png", "w": 800, "h": 562, "len": 625.0, "beak": [0.2, 0.22, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 22, "b": "AAAABgAAAAYA8AAMAfgAOAP8ADAD/gBxAf8AZwH/gMwB/8G4Af/j8AD/94AA//4AAH/8AAA//gAAH/4AAAf/AAQPh4AEPwHABfgA4A/QAHA+AAAA8AAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Pileated Woodpecker", "sci": "Dryocopus pileatus", "n": 5, "first": "8:51 AM", "last": "9:09 AM", "ts": 1793627475, "mins": [531, 532, 547, 548, 549], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "It chisels big rectangular holes in dead wood while hunting carpenter ants. Abandoned holes later shelter owls, ducks and other cavity nesters.", "vox": "sounds/voice/dryocopus-pileatus.mp3?v=1793112997", "snd": "sounds/dryocopus-pileatus.mp3?v=1793113171", "sndby": "Mike Nelson, xeno-canto XC102028, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "pileated", "cm": 45.0, "img": "cutouts/pileated.png", "w": 649, "h": 800, "len": 737.0, "beak": [0.42, 0.1, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 39, "b": "AAAf/gAAH/4AAB/+Bzwf/x/4H/9/8B///+Af/3/AH/8/8B//D/wf/wf+H/8D/x//Af+f/wP/n/8D/5//A////wH///8B////Af///wH///4A/9/+AP/f/gD/3/4Af5//AH+f/wA/v/4AP7//AB+//wAf//8AD///AAf//wAH//8ABv/+AAD//gAAf/4AAH//AAA//wAAP/8AAD/+"}}, {"name": "Tufted Titmouse", "sci": "Baeolophus bicolor", "n": 3, "first": "8:53 AM", "last": "8:57 AM", "ts": 1793627598, "mins": [533, 536, 537], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Titmice line their nests with hair, and they have been seen plucking it from live raccoons, dogs, and even people.", "vox": "sounds/voice/baeolophus-bicolor.mp3?v=1793112971", "snd": "sounds/baeolophus-bicolor.mp3?v=1793113083", "sndby": "Jim Berry, xeno-canto XC649331, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "titmouse", "cm": 16.5, "img": "cutouts/titmouse.png", "w": 574, "h": 800, "len": 653.0, "beak": [0.2, 0.25, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 45, "b": "cAAAAHAAAAAwAAAAIAAAAGgDAAB8B4AAPB8AAD5/AAA+/4AAeP+AAHv/wAB7/8AA+P/gAPhn4ACbH/AADz/wAA9/+AAPf/wABb/8AB+//gAO//4ABt//AAfP/wAHx/+AB+P/gAPh/8AHiP/AB47/wAeH/+AHofvgBvOB8AD3APgAfAB4APgAPAD4AB4A+AAfAPwADwA3AA8APwAHAD4AAAAfAAAAGwAAABgAAAAMAAAADAAA"}}, {"name": "Downy Woodpecker", "sci": "Dryobates pubescens", "n": 5, "first": "9:01 AM", "last": "11:57 AM", "ts": 1793628085, "mins": [541, 657, 715, 717, 717], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "North America’s smallest woodpecker. It is small enough to forage on thin weed stalks and twigs that bigger woodpeckers can’t cling to.", "vox": "sounds/voice/dryobates-pubescens.mp3?v=1793112995", "snd": "sounds/dryobates-pubescens.mp3?v=1793113092", "sndby": "Thomas G. Graves, xeno-canto XC89855, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "downy", "cm": 16.0, "img": "cutouts/downy.png", "w": 486, "h": 800, "len": 707.0, "beak": [0.59, 0.1, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 53, "b": "AAAAAP/4AAD//AAA//wAAP/8IAD//DwA//wf4H/8D/x//Af+f/wH/3/8A/9//AP/f/wD/3/8B/9//A//f/wf/n/8P/x//H/8f/7//H/+//x////8f////D////x////8f////H////h////4f///+D////A////wP///4D///+A////gP///wD///8A///+AP///gD///wA///4AP//+AD///AA///wAP//sAD//4AA//8AAP//AAD//wAA//4AAP/+AAD//gAA//4AAP/8AAD//AAA="}}, {"name": "American Crow", "sci": "Corvus brachyrhynchos", "n": 16, "first": "9:02 AM", "last": "2:04 PM", "ts": 1793628135, "mins": [542, 542, 546, 593, 602, 609, 637, 638, 648, 656, 715, 716, 753, 841, 841, 844], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Crows remember individual human faces for years, and they seem to pass the grudge along to other crows.", "vox": "sounds/voice/corvus-brachyrhynchos.mp3?v=1793112990", "snd": "sounds/corvus-brachyrhynchos.mp3?v=1793113335", "sndby": "David Vander Pluym, xeno-canto XC289011, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "crow", "cm": 45.0, "img": "cutouts/crow.png", "w": 553, "h": 364, "len": 568.0, "beak": [0.94, 0.14, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 21, "b": "AAAHwAAAD/gAAB/+AAB//gAD/+AAD//AAB//wAB//4AB//+AB///AAf//wAP//4AH//4AH//8AD//8AA/x+AAPgPwAbwF/6/f////3///7Bhw5AA"}}, {"name": "Red-bellied Woodpecker", "sci": "Melanerpes carolinus", "n": 3, "first": "9:09 AM", "last": "1:25 PM", "ts": 1793628562, "mins": [549, 804, 805], "anim": null, "trunk": true, "fact": "The “red belly” is only a faint pink blush you will rarely see. The red cap is the giveaway. Its barbed tongue can reach about two inches past the bill tip.", "vox": "sounds/voice/melanerpes-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113012", "snd": "sounds/melanerpes-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113332", "sndby": "Bruce Lagerquist, xeno-canto XC713255, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "redbellied", "cm": 24.0, "img": "cutouts/redbellied.png", "w": 400, "h": 800, "len": 516.0, "beak": [0.52, 0.23, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 64, "b": ""}}, {"name": "White-throated Sparrow", "sci": "Zonotrichia albicollis", "n": 1, "first": "9:10 AM", "last": "9:10 AM", "ts": 1793628626, "mins": [550], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "White-throats come in two color forms, white-striped and tan-striped, and almost always pair with the opposite form. Listen for “Oh-sweet-Canada, Canada, Canada.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/zonotrichia-albicollis.mp3?v=1793113071", "snd": "sounds/zonotrichia-albicollis.mp3?v=1793113140", "sndby": "Matt Wistrand, xeno-canto XC339648, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "whitethroated", "cm": 17.0, "img": "cutouts/whitethroated.png", "w": 800, "h": 460, "len": 599.0, "beak": [0.73, 0.14, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 18, "b": "AAB+AAAB/gAAB/8DAB/+BwB//h4B//54f//84P///8AAP/8AAA/8AAAD8AAAA/AAAA8AAAA/AAAAeAAAAeAAAAeAAAAHAAAA"}}, {"name": "Common Grackle", "sci": "Quiscalus quiscula", "n": 2, "first": "9:10 AM", "last": "9:31 AM", "ts": 1793628631, "mins": [550, 571], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its glossy head can flash purple, bronze or green. I